ilable tiles for winning:\\n   - If we keep T1,T2,T7,T8,T9, we can win by:\\n        * Drawing T3: then we have chow T1,T2,T3 (second identical chow) and chow T7,T8,T9, and pair T7? Wait, we'd have T1,T2,T3 (chow), T7,T8,T9 (chow), and we need a pair. We'd have leftover T? Actually, we'd have 6 tiles: T1,T2,T3,T7,T8,T9. That's two chows, but we already have 3 chows exposed, so total would be 5 chows? No, we need exactly 4 melds. We have 3 exposed chows. We need one more meld and a pair. If we draw T3, we can form a chow T1,T2,T3 as the 4th meld, and then we have T7,T8,T9 left \\u2014 that's three tiles, but they can't form a pair; they are a chow. That would give us 4 chows and no pair. So we need a pair. So we must convert one of the chows into a pair+single? Actually, the winning hand must be 4 melds + 1 pair. So with T1,T2,T3 chow and T7,T8,T9 chow, we would have 5 melds total (including the three exposed). Too many. So we must use one of the sets as a pair. So perhaps pair T1 and use T2,T3 +? Not straightforward.\\n   Let's think systematically:\\n\\nWe have 5 hand tiles. We need to form one meld and a pair from these 5 tiles. So the 5 tiles should be a 3-tile meld and a 2-tile pair, or possibly a 4-tile set that can be split into meld+pair? Not in standard.\\nSo the 5 tiles must be either:\\n   a) A triplet (pung) + a pair, but we have all singles.\\n   b) A chow + a pair.\\n   c) Two pairs + a single, but that's not a complete set (would need to become a pair and a chow with new tile).\\n\\nGiven our tiles are T1,T2,T7,T8,T9, the natural chows are T1,T2,T3 or T7,T8,T9. So we likely want to complete one of these chows and have a pair from the remaining two tiles.\\n\\nScenario 1: Complete T7,T8,T9 chow. Then we have T1,T2 left as a pair if we draw T1 or T2 to pair. So winning tiles: for chow T7,T8,T9, we need T7, T8, or T9 to complete the chow? Actually, T7,T8,T9 is already a chow if we have all three. We currently have one each of T7,T8,T9. So we already have 