or win anyway, but if you can't win due to points, you should avoid discarding a tile that might give others a win.  \n\nLooking at game history: Many honors and terminals have been discarded. Your opponents have melds including pungs of terminals and honors. The next player has three pungs: T6, T3, B8. The previous player has a pung of B1 and a kong of T4. The across player has a chow of W2-W4.  \n\nSafety: Discarding J1 might be safer because J1 is a dragon, and dragons are often used in high-scoring hands, but many have been discarded (J1 unshown count is 2, but from history, J1 has been played by across player and next player? Actually, history shows across played J1 once, next player didn't play J1, previous played J2 twice. So J1 might be relatively safe. T7 has been played by you and others (you played T7 once, next player played T7 once, previous played T7 once). So both are somewhat live.  \n\nGiven the scoring issue, you might not be able to win even if you draw the pair. But you must choose one action. Since you have only two options, and the game might be near end, I'd choose based on maximizing winning chances if you can somehow get 8 points. Perhaps if you draw the winning tile as the last tile or rob a kong, you could get additional points. But that's uncertain.  \n\nAlternatively, maybe your hand already has some patterns:  \n- All Chows: if pair is suited, 2 points.  \n- No Honors: 1 point.  \n- Possibly Two Terminal Chows: You have T1-T3 and T7-T9, both terminal chows (1,2,3 and 7,8,9 in same suit). That's worth 1 point (and can be counted once). So that's 2+1+1=4 points. Still short.  \n- Short Straight: You have two chows in Bamboo: T1-T3 and T7-T9 are not successive (they are separated), so not short straight.  \n- Mixed Double Chow? Not applicable.  \n- Pure Double Chow? Not applicable.  \n\nSo likely only 4 points. Not enough to win.  \n\nTherefore, your hand is not a winning hand in terms of points. You should still play, but winning is impos